import{_ as n,P as i,d as a,e as o,y as s,q as r,f as c}from"./chunks/framework.D7CAgLCq.js";const b=JSON.parse(`{"title":"A.6 HV06 Drive–Uptake Chain (Full Interface Card)","description":"","frontmatter":{"title":"A.6 HV06 Drive–Uptake Chain (Full Interface Card)","xkChars":0,"xkWords":1183,"xkLessons":{"A.6":{"title":"A.6 HV06 Drive–Uptake Chain (Complete Interface Card)","lead":"HV06 examines direction, generation, uptake, maintenance, and repayment segment by segment. Locally grounded segments, a candidate complete chain, and an effective channel are three different conclusions.","explanation":["Source location: Chinese source lines 3984–4049, original-site location /theory/appendix-a/a-06#section-a-6. The current full text is in the theory-v2 source; the historical research draft and excerpts remain downloadable in their original versions.","Short quotation translated from Chinese: “One cannot simply multiply the average effects of the edges.” HV06 asks how the chain connects segment by segment from direction and generation through execution and maintenance to repayment. Every segment needs a channel, resources, costs, responsibility, and lag, rather than treating enthusiasm, vision, or commands as evidence that resources have arrived and maintenance has someone to undertake it. [T-main]","HV06-R0-segment-map requires only that inputs, outputs, delays, losses, interruptions, and resource and cost boundaries be observable for at least one chain segment. It permits descriptions of local segments, missing bridges, and cost positions, useful even before a complete chain exists. But one visible segment or a statement that “the drive is strong” cannot establish a complete or effective channel. [T-main]","HV06-R1-complete-chain-composition adds the HV03, HV04, and HV05 interfaces and requires segment-by-segment connections within the same mapping of object, scale, window, and quantities. This is composition of a candidate chain, not causal support for every edge. If upstream meaning expression is only R0, even the complete chain must retain its candidate status; drawing connections cannot automatically upgrade H1. [T-main; T-anchor; T-generation; T-carrier]","On top of a complete candidate chain, HV06-R2-effective-channel requires qualified G2 support for the specified channel, along with same-case correspondence, common support, joint responses, temporal capacity, and terminal outcomes sufficient to support the whole-chain effect in question, plus CAUSAL/E4. Edges from different populations or windows, upstream outputs exceeding downstream capacity, or mutually incompatible success conditions can all prevent individual average edge effects from combining into a whole-chain result. [T-main; T-G2]","A necessary check for “completeness” is alignment of quantities and objects, not a sufficient number of node names. An upstream output may be service places while a downstream input requires qualified personnel at specific times; the two are not equal merely because both are written as headcounts. Temporal order must also allow upstream outputs to arrive when the next segment needs them. Late resources cannot retroactively fill harm that has already occurred. [T-main]","One successful traversal does not establish reproduction across periods, justified goals, complete responsibility, or action authorization. Informal caregivers outside the boundary may bear costs, so the apparent chain keeps operating while draining reserves; subsequent maintenance may also lack continuity. Failure does not automatically mean insufficient willingness, much less that uptake providers should fill the gaps themselves. [T-main]","Register start-up, transmission, maintenance, and repayment lags segment by segment, retaining uncertainty about cross-organizational, low-visibility, and informal positions and alternative channels. Uptake providers and affected people along the chain must be able to challenge resources, costs, and attribution safely, with review outside the original chain’s judgments and decisions. Authorized rollback should revoke whole-chain attribution and downstream effects, restoring node-level descriptions and unresolved status without deleting locally grounded material. [T-main]","GOV.UK retirement guidance separately arranges uptake of needs, notification, redirection, and data protection, helping map distinct task nodes in a candidate chain. The guidance’s existence does not prove that a particular user reached a new service or that subsequent operating budgets are in place. It serves here as an R0/R1-style design comparison; a policy flowchart is not treated as whole-chain G2 support. [P-RETIRE]","Field-by-field study of all 39 fields (“Original card” below retains the field’s complete content in translation; “Interpretation” specifies its role and irreplaceable responsibility).","01 | Interface ID (id). Original card: HV06. Interpretation: This is a short identifier within the card collection, used to track the same interface; it does not mean that a real object has been proven. [T-main]","02 | Qualified ID (qualified_id). Original card: human_variable:HV06. Interpretation: the namespace prevents naming collisions; references to this ID must still carry object and evidential status. [T-main]","03 | Name (name). Original card: Drive–uptake chain. Interpretation: the name provides a reading entry point, not an automatic label for particular people, groups, or real structures. [T-main]","04 | Claim type (claim_type). Original card: H. Interpretation: H identifies the role of a human-instance/interface proposition; it cannot be upgraded into formal proof or an unconditional law of the world. [T-main]","05 | Contract role (contract_role). Original card: human_variable_interface. Interpretation: this variable-interface contract specifies how to enter analysis; a field’s existence does not mean an empirical instance has been established. [T-main]","06 | Proposition (proposition). Original card: The chain from orientation and generation through execution, maintenance, and repayment must register channels, resources, costs, responsibilities, and lags segment by segment. Interpretation: read this proposition together with the subsequent conditional routes and ceilings; do not extract an unconditional causal or normative conclusion from one sentence. [T-main]","07 | Scope (scope). Original card: Human collective action, projects, organizations, and institutional operation. Interpretation: scope lists task domains that may be studied, not empirical support for every object within them. [T-main]","08 | Pause condition (pause_condition). Original card: Enthusiasm, visions, or orders substitute for evidence of uptake and repayment. Interpretation: pausing concerns dependent judgment or adoption; independent descriptions may remain, but failed premises cannot continue to be used. [T-main]","09 | Inferential dependencies (inferential_requires). Original card: None (empty set). Interpretation: Inferential dependencies provide the upstream content needed to establish a proposition. Upstream candidate/supported status must be carried through unchanged; citation does not automatically upgrade it. [T-main]","10 | Protocol dependencies (protocol_requires). Original card, translated from Chinese: 1. EVIDENCE; 2. SOURCE. Interpretation: Protocol dependencies constrain evidence collection and expression; completing protocols does not independently establish the truth of empirical propositions. [T-main]","11 | Qualification / specialization (specializes). Original card: None (empty set). Interpretation: an empty set means only that no such specialization relations are registered, not that evidential responsibility is absent; a nonempty set also does not imply universal validity. [T-main]","12 | Applicable-object references (applies_to). Original card: None (empty set). Interpretation: applicable-object references cannot replace the current object contract; an empty set is explicit registration, not permission to apply to every object. [T-main]","13 | Conditional support routes (conditional_support_routes). Original card: 1. route_id=HV06-R0-segment-map; claim_level=descriptive_classification; when=Inputs, outputs, delays, losses, interruptions, resources, costs, and boundaries of at least one chain segment are observable.; additional_inferential_requires=None (empty set); additional_protocol_requires=None (empty set); allowed_conclusion=Register local chain segments, missing bridges, lags, losses, interruption points, and cost positions.; result_ceiling=No claim of a complete chain or effective channel from a single segment or language of drive. 2. route_id=HV06-R1-complete-chain-composition; claim_level=descriptive_classification; when=Records for the three interfaces of orientation, generation, and uptake can be connected segment by segment within the same object, scale, window, and mapping of quantities.; additional_inferential_requires=human_variable:HV03, human_variable:HV04, human_variable:HV05; additional_protocol_requires=None (empty set); allowed_conclusion=Register a complete candidate drive–uptake chain and segment-by-segment evidence coverage.; result_ceiling=Candidate chain composition only; complete interface records do not mean each segment has causal efficacy. 3. route_id=HV06-R2-effective-channel; claim_level=mechanism_explanation; when=A complete candidate chain has been composed, an eligible G2-instance supports the specified channel, and same-case correspondence, common support, joint responses, timing, capacity, and terminal outcomes suffice to support the whole-chain effect at issue; average edge effects cannot simply be multiplied.; additional_inferential_requires=human_variable:HV03, human_variable:HV04, human_variable:HV05, G2-instance; additional_protocol_requires=CAUSAL, E4; allowed_conclusion=Register effective drive–uptake channels and mechanisms of loss and interruption within tested segments and windows.; result_ceiling=One successful traversal cannot imply reproduction across periods, responsibility, justification, or action authorization. Interpretation: R0 covers local segments; R1 connects HV03/04/05 into a candidate chain under the same object, scale, window, and quantity mapping; R2 additionally checks G2, same-case common support, joint responses, timing, capacity, and terminal outcomes. Simply multiplying edge averages is prohibited. [T-main]","14 | Allowed inference (allowed_inference). Original card: 1. Chain bottlenecks; 2. Disconnection between drive and uptake; 3. Hidden repayment. Interpretation: these are permitted directions of conclusion; which is actually reached and with what strength remain limited by the selected route and materials. [T-main]","15 | Prohibited leap (prohibited_leap). Original card: 1. Strong drive equals sustainability; 2. Attributing failure to insufficient willingness; 3. Uptake providers should fill gaps themselves. Interpretation: these are common boundary violations, not avoided by rewording; inspect actual inference and downstream use, not wording alone. [T-main]","16 | Nine-axis scale profile (scale_profile). Original card: SP=<A,X,T,O,C,R,I,N,J>; A=aggregation level: individual link events, chain cases, populations of similar chains, input/output distributions, and aggregation rules; X=spatial scope: action sites, project or organizational boundaries, digital collaboration spaces, and cross-domain spillovers; T=time span: initiation, maintenance, interruption, and recovery windows and cycles across periods; O=organizational level: initiating roles, execution teams, organizations, institutions, through governance ecosystems; C=causal level: link events, drive–resource–task interaction mechanisms, meso-level uptake chains, institutional arrangements, and systemic conditions; R=observational resolution: original task/resource records, link sequences, chain cases, loss distributions, performance indicators, and summaries, with compression loss registered; I=sphere of influence: direct initiating and uptake positions, indirect beneficiary or cost positions, second-order spillovers, cross-domain and intergenerational effects; N=network-topological scope: dependency chains, alternative paths, bottlenecks, feedback connections, and cross-domain bridges; J=jurisdiction and authorization scope: register authorization separately for goal adoption, task allocation, resource input, stopping, and experimentation; mark cross-axis positions segment by segment. Interpretation: register all nine axes individually; particularly, sphere of influence I creates no jurisdictional authority J, and lower observational resolution R requires retention of compression loss. [T-main]","17 | Effective object (effective_object). Original card: A traceable chain turning orientation and generation into sustained action. Interpretation: the traceable whole chain is the object of study; its name does not establish persistence in advance. R0 is local only, R1 candidate composition only, and R2 effects within tested windows only. [T-main]","18 | Cross-scale invariants (scale_invariants). Original card: 1. Chains of orientation, generation, uptake, resources, costs, and responsibility. Interpretation: invariants are comparative responsibilities retained during transfer, not a declaration that variable values remain constant at every scale. [T-main]","19 | Required additions on elevation (required_scale_additions). Original card: 1. Cross-level bridges; 2. Aggregation losses; 3. Responsibility inheritance; 4. Protection floors. Interpretation: elevation is not copying and enlarging the original table. Where materials on new populations, interfaces, responsibility positions, and so on are incomplete, restrict conclusions dependent on them. [T-main]","20 | Semantics changing with scale (changing_semantics). Original card: 1. Nodes, channels, and bottlenecks may change with organizational scale. Interpretation: the same word may change meaning when the object or scale changes; reconstruct correspondence rather than assume the original meaning applies unchanged. [T-main]","21 | Inapplicable objects (non_applicable_objects). Original card: 1. Nonhuman processes without intentional drive and human uptake. Interpretation: inapplicability constraints prevent unconditional projection of human normative, responsibility, and experiential interfaces; they do not deny that other theories can study these objects. [T-main]","22 | Forbidden elevation (forbidden_elevation). Original card: 1. Local drive or a single node directly representing the complete chain. Interpretation: inference from local to whole requires separate proof; broad impact, a grand name, or formal similarity cannot supply representativeness and authorization. [T-main]","23 | State set (state). Original card: 1. Connected through; 2. Lagging; 3. Overloaded; 4. Broken; 5. Replaced. Interpretation: states are descriptive classifications requiring evidence, not a maturity ladder for scoring people; state changes require windows, grounds, and versions. [T-main]","24 | Observables (observables). Original card: 1. Records of anchors becoming tasks, budgets, rules, or schedules; 2. Inputs/outputs, delays, losses, and interruption points of each segment; 3. Changes in uptake providers’ capacity, stopping, and alternative paths; 4. The chain output’s actual contribution to target outcomes. Interpretation: separate tasks, budgets, and schedules from actual terminal contributions. Record delays and capacity for every segment, allowing bridge failures to be found rather than selecting successful endpoints only. [T-main]","25 | Evidence requirements (evidence). Original card: 1. Resource flows; 2. Task and maintenance records; 3. Repayment and costs; 4. Lags. Interpretation: obtain listed evidence according to route and task relevance; distinguish unknown, uncollected, inapplicable, and unavailable for protection, without forcing disclosure to fill a table. [T-main]","26 | Input dependencies and interface content (input_dependencies). Original card: 1. Orientation anchors; 2. Generative nodes; 3. Uptake layers. Interpretation: orientation, generation, and uptake are interfaces required for complete candidate chains under R1/R2; R0 needs at least one segment only, without fabricating all interfaces to describe a local gap. [T-main]","27 | Output effects and variable flow (output_effects). Original card: 1. Action outcomes; 2. Loads; 3. Feedback and evolutionary traces. Interpretation: variable flow identifies downstream judgments that may depend on this interface; passing references guarantees neither downstream conclusions, actual repair, nor authorization. [T-main]","28 | Time window and lag (time_window_and_lag). Original card: Register initiation, transmission, maintenance, and repayment lags segment by segment. Interpretation: occurrence, observation, execution, and arrival of effects may differ in time; short-term materials cannot automatically certify extensions beyond the window. [T-main]","29 | Uncertainty (uncertainty). Original card: Record breaks, alternative channels, and repayment outside boundaries. Interpretation: direct uncertainty toward specific materials and positions; an overall confidence score cannot conceal fundamentally uncovered positions. [T-main]","30 | Local exclusion zone (local_exclusion_zone). Original card: Informal, low-visibility, and cross-organizational uptake positions. Interpretation: exclusion zones limit current inferential coverage, not delete people or facts from the world; unavailability for safety is neither consent nor absence of problems. [T-main]","31 | Affected positions (affected_positions). Original card: 1. Initiators; 2. Uptake providers; 3. Beneficiaries; 4. Cost bearers. Interpretation: register directly and indirectly affected parties together; visible members do not represent every cost bearer. [T-main]","32 | Uptake carrier (carrier). Original card: 1. Personnel and posts; 2. Procedures; 3. Budgets; 4. Infrastructure. Interpretation: a carrier is the structure or process that actually carries something; its name, completed construction, and arrival of the required function are different evidence. [T-main]","33 | Responsible party (responsible_subject). Original card: 1. Those responsible for behavior, decisions, authorization, supervision, and remedies at each node. Interpretation: identify who proposes, operates, or uses judgments; recording responsibility is not automatically a legal-liability adjudication or new authorization. [T-main]","34 | Normative status (normative_status). Original card: Chain effectiveness does not prove the goal justified. Interpretation: distinguish descriptive, normative, and bridging status according to the card. Established facts do not independently determine value rankings; normative reasons do not automatically prove empirical effects. [T-main]","35 | Judgment ceiling (judgment_ceiling). Original card: Up to explanatory or diagnostic level when evidence for the whole chain is sufficient. Interpretation: proportionate whole-chain materials permit explanation/diagnosis; R0/R1 retain local or candidate ceilings respectively, while one successful traversal under R2 still does not certify reproduction across periods. [T-main]","36 | Action ceiling (action_ceiling). Original card: This variable produces only descriptions of chain connectivity, lags, losses, interruptions, costs, and uptake needs; it authorizes no load reduction, resource adjustment, or experiment. Any real adjustment must separately pass C12, explicit runtime N premises, J authorization, and O procedures. Interpretation: the interface itself creates no real-world authority to impose dispositions; actual actions separately require checking choice, C12, explicit N, valid J, O, protections, and periods. [T-main]","37 | Counterexamples (counterexamples). Original card: 1. Strong visions and concentrated mobilization lack sustained resources, maintenance, or repayment; 2. An apparently connected chain transfers key costs to uptake providers outside its boundary. Interpretation: counterexamples restrict unconditional extrapolation; they may require narrowing scope or gathering evidence anew, without automatically negating every bounded use. [T-main]","38 | Appeal (appeal). Original card: Under appeal_and_rollback_rule, uptake or affected positions along the chain may challenge resources, costs, and chain attribution through safely accessible channels protected against retaliation, triggering review independent of the original chain judgment or decision chain. Interpretation: appeals must be safely accessible, free from retaliation, and capable of triggering independent review; a name alone does not mean authority to correct errors exists. [T-main]","39 | Rollback (rollback). Original card: Under appeal_and_rollback_rule, when rollback is authorized, a designated responsible person must actually revoke whole-chain attribution and its downstream effects within the specified period, restore node-level descriptions and unresolved status, and preserve versions and completion verification. Interpretation: actually cancel erroneous effects within valid authorization, correct downstream uses, and verify completion; changing document wording does not restore rights and resources to the affected person. [T-main]"],"argumentSteps":["Find observable chain segments and separately register inputs/outputs, resources and costs, delays, and boundaries.","R0 retains local facts and missing bridges without first inventing the whole chain.","R1 connects HV03/04/05 under the same object, scale, window, and quantity mapping.","R2 separately checks G2, same-case joint conditions, timing and capacity, and actual terminal outcomes.","Separate a one-time channel effect from persistence, responsibility, justification, and authorization.","If a whole-chain conclusion fails, revoke its overall effect, retain grounded nodes and unresolved status, and protect uptake providers."],"distinctions":[{"leftTitle":"R0 local segment","leftText":"An input/output relation is observable.","rightTitle":"R1 candidate whole chain","rightText":"Three interfaces connect segment by segment on matching terms."},{"leftTitle":"Candidate composition","leftText":"Records and mappings are complete.","rightTitle":"R2 effective channel","rightText":"Requires proportionate evidence of joint conditions and terminal outcomes."},{"leftTitle":"Single-edge average","leftText":"Average sample performance for one edge.","rightTitle":"Whole-chain joint behavior","rightText":"Same-case correspondence, timing, capacity, and common support cannot be omitted."},{"leftTitle":"Strong drive","leftText":"Evident enthusiasm, vision, or mobilization.","rightTitle":"Sustainable uptake","rightText":"Maintenance, repayment, and resource continuity require separate evidence."}],"boundaries":["Original-card counterexample: strong visions and mobilization without sustained resources, maintenance, and repayment.","Original-card counterexample: apparent connectivity transfers costs to uptake providers outside the boundary.","Teaching counterexample: declaring a 25% whole-chain rate because each of two edges has 50%, without checking same-case joint conditions.","Boundary in the original: chain effectiveness proves neither goal justification nor authorization."],"questions":["If only one segment is known, can nothing be said?","If HV03/04/05 interfaces all exist, is the whole chain effective?","Why can multiplying individual-edge averages be wrong?","Must all node materials be deleted when the whole-chain judgment is withdrawn?"],"diagramPath":"/reading-assets/S-A-6/figures/hv06-routes.png","caseLinks":[{"title":"Different links in service-retirement guidance","href":"https://www.gov.uk/service-manual/agile-delivery/retiring-your-service"}],"caseNotes":[{"title":"Different links in service-retirement guidance","text":"GOV.UK’s retirement guidance separately addresses subsequent needs, user notification, redirects, and information protection. It supplies identifiable task links, not a substitute called a single “shutdown” action.","sourceLabel":"GOV.UK Service Manual：Retiring your service","sourceUrl":"https://www.gov.uk/service-manual/agile-delivery/retiring-your-service","sourceLocator":"Consider user needs; Telling your users; Make a plan to redirect traffic; Protecting information; web page lines 72–165","boundary":"Based only on design guidance; proves neither continuous service for any retired service’s users, whole-chain G2, nor established long-term maintenance.","href":"https://www.gov.uk/service-manual/agile-delivery/retiring-your-service"}],"sourceRefs":[{"section":"A.6","start_line":4077,"end_line":4142,"startLine":4077,"endLine":4142}],"outputId":"section-a-6","sourceHash":"51795bb7988f2669a324a52666c320cfba1382f597f7b5c6a1431844572da1fb","teachingExamples":[{"title":"Teaching construction: two 50% rates do not guarantee 25% for the whole chain","text":"In 100 constructed same-case records, the first 50 succeed at segment one and fail at segment two; the last 50 fail at segment one and succeed at segment two. Each segment has a 50% success rate, but both succeed in 0/100=0% of the same cases, not 50%×50%=25%. This example only shows that marginal success rates cannot be multiplied unconditionally. Success rates are not causal effects either; formal G2 whole-chain judgments additionally require channel comparisons, capacity, timing, and endpoints.","assumptions":"A constructed table of complementary events, with no actual observations or randomized causal study.","boundary":"Does not deny that probabilities can factorize under explicit conditions; it prohibits using disproportionate edge averages as whole-chain effects."},{"title":"Teaching construction: resources arrive late and no one maintains them","text":"A fictional project needs 10 units of service capacity on Monday, but procurement supplies them only on Friday, and no maintenance budget is registered. Procurement commitments and actual arrival may be recorded separately; Friday’s 10 units cannot be backfilled as Monday’s fulfilled need, nor can one launch imply subsequent maintenance. Care costs outside the boundary during the wait are listed separately, not attributed to weak willingness among uptake providers.","assumptions":"Dates, capacity, and project are constructed.","boundary":"Recording gaps authorizes no procurement, resource reallocation, or demands that others fill them."}],"counterexamples":[{"identity":"source_counterexample","text":"Original-card counterexample: strong visions and mobilization without sustained resources, maintenance, and repayment.","effect":"Limits unconditional extension of this section without negating independently grounded bounded judgments.","sourceIds":["T-main"]},{"identity":"source_counterexample","text":"Original-card counterexample: apparent connectivity transfers costs to uptake providers outside the boundary.","effect":"Limits unconditional extension of this section without negating independently grounded bounded judgments.","sourceIds":["T-main"]},{"identity":"constructed_boundary","text":"Teaching counterexample: declaring a 25% whole-chain rate because each of two edges has 50%, without checking same-case joint conditions.","effect":"Limits unconditional extension of this section without negating independently grounded bounded judgments.","sourceIds":["T-main"]},{"identity":"source_counterexample","text":"Boundary in the original: chain effectiveness proves neither goal justification nor authorization.","effect":"Limits unconditional extension of this section without negating independently grounded bounded judgments.","sourceIds":["T-main"]}],"questionAnswers":[{"question":"If only one segment is known, can nothing be said?","answer":"R0 permits registration of local segments, missing bridges, and costs, but not a declaration of a complete chain.","basis":"R0。"},{"question":"If HV03/04/05 interfaces all exist, is the whole chain effective?","answer":"Only R1 candidate composition; R2 additionally requires causal and joint-support conditions.","basis":"R1/R2。"},{"question":"Why can multiplying individual-edge averages be wrong?","answer":"Same-case joint behavior, common support, timing, and capacity may not match.","basis":"R2 and the constructed complementary-event example."},{"question":"Must all node materials be deleted when the whole-chain judgment is withdrawn?","answer":"No; revoke overall effects through valid procedures and restore node descriptions and unresolved status.","basis":"rollback。"}],"figures":[{"path":"/reading-assets/S-A-6/figures/hv06-routes.png","alt":"R0 local segments, R1 complete candidate composition, R2 G2 and joint conditions; one successful traversal does not extrapolate to the long term or authorization.","caption":"Based on the three complete routes in A.6."},{"path":"/reading-assets/S-A-6/figures/hv06-composition.png","alt":"Whole-chain assessment must check same-case common support, mappings of input/output quantities, timing and capacity, terminal outcomes, and repayment outside boundaries.","caption":"Based on A.6 R2 and cost protection; see the main text for the 50% example."}],"diagramBrief":"The three-route diagram shows evidential layers rather than a promotion ladder; the whole-chain verification diagram shows same-case quantity mappings, timing and capacity, terminal outcomes, and external costs.","applicability":{"realCase":"applicable","diagram":"applicable","reason":"This unit concerns methodological judgment. Actual sources support only the listed facts; the diagram and teaching construction each retain their own status."},"openQuestions":["No actual G2 whole-chain instance; constructed joint success rates are not empirical causal results.","Same-case matching, support ranges, quantity mappings, terminal outcomes, and costs outside boundaries require measurement and safe review."],"taskId":"S-A-6","sources":[{"id":"T-main","title":"Historical research manuscript: Xikari, On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation","url":"/reading-assets/theory.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"manuscript-20261013-df579dbdbb68; the text dates itself to October 2026","locator":"§A.6, Chinese source lines 3898–3963","claim_support":["All 39 HV06 fields and three routes; whole-chain joint conditions."],"limits":["Supports the current manuscript's textual meaning and explicitly bounded inference; does not by itself establish empirical universality, implementation effects or readers' learning outcomes.","This entry preserves the original research’s version, locations, and reading scope; it is not a record of external-source review for v2. For the current theory, see theory-v2."],"access_status":"read"},{"id":"T-anchor","title":"Historical research manuscript: Xikari, On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation","url":"/reading-assets/theory.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"manuscript-20261013-df579dbdbb68; the text dates itself to October 2026","locator":"§A.3, Chinese source lines 3700–3765","claim_support":["Evidential status of candidate/effective meaning anchors."],"limits":["Supports the current manuscript's textual meaning and explicitly bounded inference; does not by itself establish empirical universality, implementation effects or readers' learning outcomes.","This entry preserves the original research’s version, locations, and reading scope; it is not a record of external-source review for v2. For the current theory, see theory-v2."],"access_status":"read"},{"id":"T-generation","title":"Historical research manuscript: Xikari, On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation","url":"/reading-assets/theory.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"manuscript-20261013-df579dbdbb68; the text dates itself to October 2026","locator":"§A.4, historical Chinese research manuscript lines 3766–3831","claim_support":["GC/GS/GE candidates and mechanism classification."],"limits":["Supports the current manuscript's textual meaning and explicitly bounded inference; does not by itself establish empirical universality, implementation effects or readers' learning outcomes.","This entry preserves the original research’s version, locations, and reading scope; it is not a record of external-source review for v2. For the current theory, see theory-v2."],"access_status":"read"},{"id":"T-carrier","title":"Historical research manuscript: Xikari, On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation","url":"/reading-assets/theory.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"manuscript-20261013-df579dbdbb68; the text dates itself to October 2026","locator":"§A.5, Chinese historical source lines 3832–3897","claim_support":["Uptake, responsibility, and boundaries across periods."],"limits":["Supports the current manuscript's textual meaning and explicitly bounded inference; does not by itself establish empirical universality, implementation effects or readers' learning outcomes.","This entry preserves the original research’s version, locations, and reading scope; it is not a record of external-source review for v2. For the current theory, see theory-v2."],"access_status":"read"},{"id":"T-G2","title":"Historical research manuscript: Xikari, On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation","url":"/reading-assets/theory.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"manuscript-20261013-df579dbdbb68; the text dates itself to October 2026","locator":"§4.4, Chinese source lines 610–619","claim_support":["Specified channel effects."],"limits":["Supports the current manuscript's textual meaning and explicitly bounded inference; does not by itself establish empirical universality, implementation effects or readers' learning outcomes.","This entry preserves the original research’s version, locations, and reading scope; it is not a record of external-source review for v2. For the current theory, see theory-v2."],"access_status":"read"},{"id":"P-RETIRE","title":"GOV.UK Service Manual：Retiring your service","url":"https://www.gov.uk/service-manual/agile-delivery/retiring-your-service","source_type":"primary","version_or_date":"Published 2016-08-04; page updated 2025-08-26","locator":"Consider user needs; Telling your users; Make a plan to redirect traffic; Protecting information; web page lines 72–165","claim_support":["Retirement design separately considers uptake of needs, notification and redirection to new services, and information protection."],"limits":["The researcher read these sections. They are design requirements, not proof that a particular service’s whole chain is effective, all users reached their destinations, or future operation will persist; they are not formal G2 instances."],"access_status":"partially_read"},{"id":"theory-v2","title":"Xikari: On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation, v2, November 2026","url":"/downloads/Xikari-条件结构与转化论.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"v2 / 2026-11-16","locator":"A.6 HV06 Drive–uptake chain (complete interface card); Chinese source lines 3984–4049; SHA-256 6ef2e508474d4ac1ad07d7e4e068955342c405b5f680afcfc40d68cca3d0149c","claim_support":["The current source section and this text’s conceptual explanation of v2; not a substitute for external empirical materials."],"limits":["Older research attachments retain their original manuscript identity; the previous scope of external reading has not expanded because of this edition change."],"access_status":"read"}],"lecturePath":"/reading-assets/S-A-6/lecture.md","sourcesPath":"/reading-assets/S-A-6/sources.json","sourceSlicePath":"/reading-assets/S-A-6/source/S-A-6.md","supplementaryFiles":[],"diagramsPath":"/reading-assets/S-A-6/diagrams.md","editionNote":"This page’s source text and section locations correspond to v2; historical research attachments and prior external-reading records retain their own versions and scopes.","translationOf":{"language":"zh","lesson_sha256":"f972e3924127ad7dae620ae62a3b215eb0088bdbc058be6607e0e6a9017452a3","source_sha256":"6ef2e508474d4ac1ad07d7e4e068955342c405b5f680afcfc40d68cca3d0149c"}}}},"headers":[],"relativePath":"en/theory/appendix-a/a-06.md","filePath":"en/theory/appendix-a/a-06.md"}`),d={name:"en/theory/appendix-a/a-06.md"};function l(u,e,p,h,m,g){const t=i("SectionLesson");return a(),o("div",null,[e[0]||(e[0]=s("",1)),r(t,{section:"A.6"}),e[1]||(e[1]=c("div",{class:"theory-source"},null,-1))])}const v=n(d,[["render",l]]);export{b as __pageData,v as default};
